Rajendra Lama’s phone has barely stopped buzzing. In between coordinating rescue operations for flood survivors with the .css-jfmysa{display:inline;word-break:break-word;} .css-1ankfgb{cursor:pointer;display:inline;word-break:break-word;} Nepal Mountaineering Association and running his travel company, he spends his days communicating with anxious tour groups, trying to persuade them not to cancel their trips.

“We have a crisis in one part of the country, but we’re open elsewhere and are not affected by the floods,” said Lama, a tourism entrepreneur with over 20 years of experience. “I’m just trying to communicate that to my clients.”

Catastrophic flooding along the Nepal-China border on August 26 grabbed global headlines, as tsunami-like waves – a mix of water, sediment and boulders – raced down from the Himalayas, submerging villages more than 100km (62 miles) downstream across northern and central Nepal.

